      "sourceBasis": {
        "kind": "derived",
        "step": "Theorem 6 gives W[1]-hardness through XNLP-hardness for pathwidth divided by the logarithm of the graph order. For a Clique instance of length M and parameter k, the reduction therefore produces a graph of pathwidth at most g(k)(1+log₂ M) for a computable g. Treewidth satisfies the same upper bound. Using the effective cutoff with accuracy 1/g(k) bounds the exponential factor by a computable function of k times M. The resulting Clique algorithm would be fixed-parameter tractable. Theorem 11 of the IPEC paper gives the width bound directly for Independent Set; the solver can ignore the supplied tree decomposition."
      },
      "sources": [
        {
          "editionId": "journal",
          "locator": "§4.3, Theorem 6 (logarithmic pathwidth)",
          "paperId": "bodlaender2025-linear-structure",
          "placement": "statement",
          "role": "logarithmic pathwidth hardness"
        },
        {
          "editionId": "proceedings",
          "locator": "Theorem 11 (logarithmic treewidth).",
          "paperId": "bodlaender2022-tree-structured",
          "placement": "statement",
          "role": "unrestricted lower bound"
        }
      ],
      "statement": "Under FPT ≠ W[1], no deterministic algorithm decides Independent Set within the stated running time.",
      "status": "established",
